          "heading": "What Is a Political Party?",
          "para": "A political party is a group of people who come together to contest elections and hold power in the government. Three features define it: parties agree on some policies and programmes for society, seeking to implement them by winning popular support through elections; parties are a link between society and government - they reflect competing views and put them before voters as a package; and a party seeks to form government - unlike pressure groups, whose members may agree on aims but do not contest elections as an organisation. Membership matters too: parties have members who accept the party's programme and work for it, from grassroots workers to leaders. Parties are also partisan by design - each is known for the part of society and the set of ideas it stands for, and this partisanship is healthy because it turns differences into clear, choosable alternatives. In a modern democracy, parties are not optional machinery - they are the vehicle through which society's divisions and demands become organised political choices. That is why scholars call parties the central institutions of representative democracy: no parties, no collective responsibility, no coherent government.",
